## Migration: `TimedExecutor` → .NET 8

Your code uses the **timeout enforcement** pattern: spawn a thread, `Join(timeout)`, then `Thread.Abort()` if it exceeds the limit. In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — it simply doesn't work.

### The Problem

| Removed API | Why it fails |
|---|---|
| `thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (`SYSLIB0006`) |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Never thrown — dead code |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Also throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException on timeout
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }
}
```

If the `work` delegate itself should respond to cancellation (so it actually **stops** instead of running to completion in the background), accept and forward a token:

```csharp
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    var token = cts.Token;
    var task = Task.Run(() => work(token), token);

    try
    {
        return task.WaitAsync(token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        throw new TimeoutException(
            $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
}
```

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Cancellation isn't immediate** | Unlike `Thread.Abort()`, cooperative cancellation only works at checkpoints. The `work` delegate must call `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` in loops or between expensive operations, or it will run to completion in the background. |
| **Fire-and-forget background work** | If `work` ignores the token, `WaitAsync` returns on timeout but the task keeps running silently on a thread pool thread. This is a resource leak the old `Abort()` approach didn't have. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | `CTS` is `IDisposable` — leaking it causes timer leaks. Always wrap in `using`. |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException`** | Only catch it at the top-level orchestration point (here, to convert to `TimeoutException`). Let it propagate everywhere else. |
| **Blocking I/O won't respond** | Synchronous calls like `socket.Receive()` or `Thread.Sleep()` won't check the token. Replace with async overloads (`await stream.ReadAsync(buf, token)`) or `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)`. |

### Async Version (Preferred)

If callers can go async, this is the cleanest approach:

```csharp
public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
    Func<CancellationToken, Task<T>> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    try
    {
        return await work(cts.Token);
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        throw new TimeoutException(
            $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
}
```

The fundamental shift: **you can no longer kill work from the outside**. The work must cooperate by checking the `CancellationToken`. If the work is truly uncooperative (e.g., third-party code you can't modify), the only option is to move it to a **child process** and use `Process.Kill()`.
